course: french
lesson: 35
title: False friends
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Many French words look just like English ones and mean the same, like animal or table - but some look friendly and then trick you: these are called false friends (faux amis).
explain: Raisin looks like raisin, but in French it means a juicy grape; a librairie looks like a library, but it is a bookshop; and attendre looks like attend, but it means to wait.
explain: Picture breakfast in a French hotel: you want jam, so you try to make up a word from English "preserve" - and your teacher jumps in fast, because that word, préservatif, is a famously embarrassing false friend that does not mean jam at all; the word you want is confiture.
explain: The safe habit is simple: when a French word looks too easy, check it before you use it.
explain: A false friend can still be a good friend once you know it: un car is a coach, la monnaie is your change, and la lecture is reading time, not a boring talk.
fact: Faux amis, false friends, are French words that look like English words but mean something different.
fact: Le raisin means grapes; a dried raisin is un raisin sec.
fact: Jam is la confiture; préservatif is an embarrassing false friend and does not mean jam.
fact: Sensible in French means sensitive (easily upset or touched); "sensible" in English is raisonnable or sage.
fact: Attendre means to wait: J'attends le bus means "I'm waiting for the bus".
fact: Le collège is secondary school for ages 11 to 15, le lycée is for ages 15 to 18, and university is l'université or la fac.
fact: Un car is a coach for trips; a car is une voiture and a city bus is un bus.
fact: La monnaie means change or coins; money in general is l'argent.
fact: Blessé means hurt: "Tu es blessé ?" means "Are you hurt?", and une blessure is an injury.
fact: To say you are full after a meal, French people say "Je n'ai plus faim" (I'm not hungry any more); "je suis plein" sounds odd.
fact: La location means rental or hire: location de vélos means bike hire; a place is un endroit or un lieu.
fact: La lecture means reading; a long talk is une conférence or un cours.
exercise: What are faux amis? | Words that look English but mean something else | French words that mean exactly the same in English | Friends who pretend to speak French | Rude words that you should never say
exercise: Aisha's penpal ate du raisin. What did she eat? | Grapes | Raisins | Prunes | Plums
exercise: How do you say a dried raisin in French? | un raisin sec | un raisin | une prune | un raisin frais
exercise: Theo wants jam at breakfast. What should he ask for? | de la confiture | du préservatif | de la marmelade anglaise | du jam
exercise: Grace's penpal says her sister is très sensible. What does that mean? | Her sister is very sensitive | Her sister is very sensible | Her sister is very good at sport | Her sister is very strict
exercise: How do you say "sensible" (wise, careful) in French? | raisonnable or sage | sensible | sensitif | sentimental
exercise: Leo's friend says "J'attends le bus." What is he doing? | Waiting for the bus | Going to a meeting on the bus | Driving the bus | Missing the bus
exercise: Olivia's French friend, aged 11, goes to le collège. What is it? | Secondary school | University | Nursery | A sports club
exercise: Which school is for ages 15 to 18 in France? | le lycée | le collège | l'école primaire | la fac
exercise: Josh's class goes to the castle "en car". How do they travel? | By coach | By family car | By train | On foot
exercise: The baker gives Ruby "votre monnaie". What does Ruby get? | Her change | All her money back | A free cake | Her receipt
exercise: What is the French for "money" in general? | l'argent | la monnaie | le change | la banque
exercise: Charlie's host asks "Tu es blessé ?" What does she want to know? | If he is hurt | If he sneezed | If he is blessed | If he is tired
exercise: After a big lunch, what does Max say politely? | Merci, je n'ai plus faim | Je suis plein, merci | J'ai faim, merci | Je suis fini, merci
exercise: Ella sees "Location de vélos". What is it? | Bike hire | Where bikes are made | A bike race | A bike repair shop
exercise: What is la lecture? | Reading | A long boring talk | A letter | A library
exercise: ★★★ How many false friends are in "J'attends le car devant la librairie avec ma monnaie" (I'm waiting for the coach outside the bookshop with my change)? | 4 | 3 | 2 | 5
test: Your mate writes to a French pal, "Raisins are dry, innit?" when she said she ate du raisin. What's the mix-up? | She ate fresh grapes | She ate a sort of pudding | She ate dried plums | Nothing, they're the same
test: ★★ Theo is about to ask for "préservatif" at breakfast. Why does his teacher stop him fast? | It's an embarrassing word, not jam | It's the word for marmalade, which he hates | It's the word for butter, not jam | It's a very posh word nobody uses
test: Grace from Glasgow writes back, "Aye, sensible folk are great," about a sister who is très sensible. What did the penpal mean? | Her sister gets upset easily | Her sister is wise and careful | Her sister is good at sport | Her sister is very bossy
test: Leo from Sydney goes, "Attending the bus? Like a meeting? Yeah nah." What should he understand? | His friend is waiting for the bus | His friend is going to a bus meeting | His friend is driving the bus | His friend hates the bus
test: Olivia from Ohio goes, "You're in college already? You're like eleven, no cap." What's true? | Collège is secondary school | French kids go to university at 11 | Collège is a summer camp | Collège is a sports club
test: ★★ Josh from Leeds hears "en car" and goes, "All thirty of us in one car? Mad, like." What will really happen? | They'll travel by coach | They'll squeeze into one car | They'll walk to the castle | They'll take the city bus
test: Ruby from Belfast hears "Voici votre monnaie" and goes, "Money? I gave her the money!" What's happening? | The baker is giving her change | The baker wants more money | The baker is refusing the money | The baker is giving her a free cake
test: Charlie from Kent hears "Tu es blessé ?" after falling off his bike and says "Bless you!" What should he have answered? | Oui, j'ai mal au genou | À tes souhaits | Non, je suis plein | Merci, je n'ai plus faim
test: ★★ Max from Manchester says "Je suis plein !" after lunch and his host laughs. What would sound natural? | Je n'ai plus faim | J'ai encore très faim | Je suis faim | Je suis vide
test: ★★★ How many of these are false friends? la librairie, le raisin, la table, la lecture, l'animal | Three | Two | Four | Five
test: What would happen if a tourist went into a "Location de vélos" to ask where the bikes were made? | They'd find out it's a place to hire bikes | They'd get a tour of a bike factory | They'd be shown a map of bike shops | They'd be sent to a museum of bikes
test: Noah from Ohio hears "la lecture" and groans, "A lecture? I thought we were reading a story!" What's true? | It means reading: story time | La lecture is a long, boring talk | La lecture is a school test | La lecture is a letter to parents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a "faux ami"? | A French word resembling an English one but differing in sense | A French word identical in form and meaning to an English word | An English word with no French equivalent | A word of no fixed meaning
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the French noun "raisin". | Grape | Dried grape | Plum | Cherry
exam: Determine the meaning of the verb "attendre". | To wait | To attend | To listen | To arrive
exam: Identify the correct French term for the coins returned to a purchaser after payment. | la monnaie | l'argent | la location | le collège
